```

This ensures that the position always has a valid time, even if the fix time wasn't set during the decoding process. This should resolve the issue with the year being less than 2000 in some cases.

Based on the error message, it appears that the issue is related to parsing the date. The error suggests that the year is not being correctly interpreted as being after 1999. Here's a fixed version of the `decodeTransparent` function that addresses this issue:

```java
private Position decodeTransparent(DeviceSession deviceSession, ByteBuf buf) {

    int type = buf.readUnsignedByte();

    if (type == 0xF0) {
        Position position = new Position(getProtocolName());
        position.setDeviceId(deviceSession.getDeviceId());

        Date time = readDate(buf, deviceSession.get(DeviceSession.KEY_TIMEZONE));

        // Ensure the year is interpreted as being after 2000
        Calendar calendar = Calendar.getInstance();
        calendar.setTime(time);
        if (calendar.get(Calendar.YEAR) < 2000) {
            calendar.add(Calendar.YEAR, 100);
            time = calendar.getTime();
        }

        if (buf.readUnsignedByte() > 0) {
            position.set(Position.KEY_ARCHIVE, true);
        }

        buf.readUnsignedByte(); // vehicle type

        int count;
        int subtype = buf.readUnsignedByte();
