Based on the error message, it seems the issue is related to parsing the time. The function is not correctly decoding the timestamp, resulting in a time value that's more than 25 hours in the future. Here's a modified version of the function that should correctly decode the Iridium position:

```java
private Position decodeIridiumPosition(Channel channel, SocketAddress remoteAddress, ByteBuf buf) {

    buf.readUnsignedShortLE(); // length

    buf.skipBytes(3); // identification header
    buf.readUnsignedIntLE(); // index

    DeviceSession deviceSession = getDeviceSession(
            channel, remoteAddress, buf.readSlice(15).toString(StandardCharsets.US_ASCII));
    if (deviceSession == null) {
        return null;
    }

    Position position = new Position(getProtocolName());
    position.setDeviceId(deviceSession.getDeviceId());

    buf.readUnsignedByte(); // session status
    buf.skipBytes(4); // reserved

    // Decode timestamp (4 bytes, little-endian, Unix timestamp)
    long timestamp = buf.readUnsignedIntLE();
    position.setTime(new Date(timestamp * 1000L));

    // Decode coordinates
    double latitude = buf.readIntLE() / 1000000.0;
    double longitude = buf.readIntLE() / 1000000.0;
    position.setLatitude(latitude);
    position.setLongitude(longitude);

    buf.skipBytes(11); // skip remaining bytes in coordinates block

    buf.skipBytes(3); // data tag header
    decodeMinimalDataSet(position, buf);

    return position;
}
```

This modified version makes the following changes:

1. It properly decodes the timestamp from the 4-byte Unix timestamp in the message.
2. It decodes the latitude and longitude from the coordinates block instead of skipping it entirely.

These changes should resolve the time-related error and provide more accurate position information.

